I don't think so, at least nothing yet.  I'm reviewing some literature with my advisor to prepare for a simulation study comparing methods of model-evaluation with missing data, and she wondered whether any software packages used an "adjusted" or "effective" sample-size instead of nrow(data) for RMSEA.

We're also curious whether you have any plans to include Bollen-Stine bootstrap capabilities when there are missing data, using either of the promising techniques proposed by Savalei & Yuan (2009) -- attached.  These are among the methods we'd like to consider, but their software <http://www2.psych.ubc.ca/~vsavalei/> support is merely running EQS, importing the results into R, and running the algorithms manually.  (Of course, this is easily adaptable by extracting the same information from the lavaan object, so we can certainly still utilize their R syntax for our simulation.)  Mplus does bootstrapping when there are missing data, but the manual merely says that "Residual bootstrap is the Bollen-Stine bootstrap" -- which I'm not sure is the case, but regardless, I can't find any other details on their site discussing how they implement it.

Anyway, no need for any changes, I was just curious.  I probably should have just searched your source code for the answer about RMSEA to save you time, but I had just posted another question about the baseline model, and I had this on my mind.

Thanks!

Terry

